issue.body:
Hello !

I am working with an autopilot communicating with MAVSDK. 
In the constructor of my Autopilot component, I want to create an "Info" plugin (and some other ones).
Unfortunately, the program **blocks** upon my component construction.

After a first investigation, I can see that the following happens : 

1) Heartbeat is received in MAVSDK => component is discovered
2) Connection is being enabled, and plugins start enabling too.
3) Info plugin is enabling, and is waiting to receive autopilot version and flight information...
(let's consider only autopilot version at first)

_Extract from [info_impl.cpp](https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/main/src/mavsdk/plugins/info/info_impl.cpp) :_
```
void InfoImpl::enable()
{
    // We can't rely on System to request the autopilot_version,
    // so we do it here, anyway.
    _system_impl->send_autopilot_version_request();
    _system_impl->send_flight_information_request();
...
...
...
void SystemImpl::send_autopilot_version_request()
{
    auto prom = std::promise<MavlinkCommandSender::Result>();
    auto fut = prom.get_future();

    send_autopilot_version_request_async(
        [&prom](MavlinkCommandSender::Result result, float) { prom.set_value(result); });

    if (fut.get() == MavlinkCommandSender::Result::Unsupported) {
        _old_message_520_supported = false;
        LogWarn() << "Trying alternative command (512).";
        send_autopilot_version_request();
    }
...
...
(same principle for send_flight_information_request)
```
4) The `std::promise` is waiting to receive and process an AUTOPILOT VERSION message,
BUT, 
by logging the thread ID, I understood that the thread that is processing HEARTBEAT, and enabling plugins is the same that is suppose to process any other messages, including AUTOPILOT VERSION.

**=> As a conclusion**, the async command is waiting to receive a message that cannot be processed, because the message reception is normally handled by the current thread, which is currently processing HEARTBEAT.


**Related information :** 
- I was not able to reproduce it with some examples provided.
- I run MAVSDK v2.6.0, but I encounter the issue since v2.0.0. I am using an UDP connection.
- I am using Ubuntu 22.04.
- I was able to find a workaround for my case, by passing `nullptr` to `send_autopilot_version_request_async`, and ignoring the handling of MAVLink msgId 520 (as mentionned in the code comments, MAVLINK is now recent enough to use REQUEST_MESSAGE instead). And I did the same thing for flight information request.

comments.body:
The code I am using is a bit complex and implies some other classes, but I was able to write a simple example that reproduce the issue: 

```cpp
#include <mavsdk.h>
#include <mavsdk/plugins/info/info.h>
#include <mavsdk/plugins/telemetry/telemetry.h>
#include <system.h>
#include <thread>

class MyComponent {
public:
  MyComponent(std::shared_ptr<mavsdk::System> system)
      : telemetry_(std::make_shared<mavsdk::Telemetry>(system)),
        // Blocking on info plugin enabling step
        info_(std::make_shared<mavsdk::Info>(system)) { 
    fprintf(stdout, "AUTOPILOT connected\n");
  }

private:
  std::shared_ptr<mavsdk::Telemetry> telemetry_;
  std::shared_ptr<mavsdk::Info> info_;
};

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
  (void)argc;
  (void)argv;

  auto my_mavsdk = std::make_shared<mavsdk::Mavsdk>(
      mavsdk::Mavsdk::Configuration{1, MAV_COMP_ID_ONBOARD_COMPUTER, true});
  my_mavsdk->add_any_connection("udp://127.0.0.1:14580");

  auto system = my_mavsdk->first_autopilot(3.0).value();

  my_mavsdk->subscribe_on_new_system([my_mavsdk, system]() {
    auto my_component = std::make_shared<MyComponent>(system);
    (void)my_component;
  });

  while (true) {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(10));
  }
  return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This is a "quick and dirty" draft, but I reproduces the issue with it, and PX4 Autopilot v1.14 SITL.

`"AUTOPILOT connected\n"` is never reached with that code. But if Info plugin construction is commented in the code (line 12), it works.

Ok, thanks for the example.

The way you do the discovery seems a bit odd to me:

```
auto system = my_mavsdk->first_autopilot(3.0).value();
```

I assume you're aware that this will give you a `nullptr` if it times out and doesn't discover an autopilot.

```
my_mavsdk->subscribe_on_new_system([my_mavsdk, system]() {
    auto my_component = std::make_shared<MyComponent>(system);
    (void)my_component;
  });
```

Why do you use `subscribe_on_new_system` when you have already waited for an autopilot? It's usually either or, so either you just wait for the first autopilot, or you need more fine grained control and you use `subscribe_on_new_system` to discover any system as they connect.

Here are two example ways to do this discovery:

Using `first_autopilot()`:

https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/324b64543d5ec1ad3d480b6d8a13d6cdc67ee68b/examples/takeoff_and_land/takeoff_and_land.cpp#L44-L52

And using `subscribe_on_new_system()`:

https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/324b64543d5ec1ad3d480b6d8a13d6cdc67ee68b/examples/fly_multiple_drones/fly_multiple_drones.cpp#L104-L123

or:

https://github.com/mavlink/MAVSDK/blob/324b64543d5ec1ad3d480b6d8a13d6cdc67ee68b/examples/camera_zoom/camera_zoom.cpp#L47-L60

